  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires finding a step function that minimizes the maximum overlap with its shifted versions. The key insight is that an optimal solution likely consists of periodic patterns with harmonic frequencies. We'll generate candidate solutions by harmonizing multiple sine waves with carefully chosen frequencies and amplitudes, then use optimization to refine these candidates further. This Fourier-inspired method can escape local optima by leveraging global structure.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent the function h(x) using a Fourier basis:\n\nh(x) = 0.5 + \u03c3_{k=1}^{NF} a_k cos(k * \u03c0 * x) + b_k sin(k * \u03c0 * x)\n\nwhere:\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n- \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n- The overlap integral C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nThe parameters {a_k, b_k} form a vector \u03b8 with manifold constraints. We optimize using projected gradient ascent on the negative overlap metric, ensuring the solution space is continuous but not necessarily convex.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with random normal coefficients for multiple low-frequency harmonics\n   - Project to satisfy 0\u2264h\u22641 and normalization\n   - Compute initial overlap\n\n2. Optimization:\n   - Maintain a population of candidate solutions with different phase sums\n   - For each, compute the overlap metric and derivative via finite differences\n   - Update using ADAM with step size decreasing with time\n   - Apply periodicity projection after each update\n\n3. Candidate generation:\n   - Regularly inject new candidates with amplitude/duration shifts\n   - Include a fallback from the provided `initial_h_values` parsed as base function\n   - Use L2 projection for harmonic computations\n\n4. Final polish:\n   - Convert Fourier coefficients to piecewise linear form\n   - Validate against discrete integrator\n   - Estimate continuous integral for safeguard\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Base frequency: 0.3 to minimize granular overlap\n- Number of harmonics: 8\n- Initial standard deviation: 0.2 (log scale)\n- Weights distribution: linear decay with frequency\n- Projection method: recursive clipping+sampling\n- Optimization: probabilistic timestep selection\n\nComputational budget and robustness:\n- Total time: ~1000 seconds\n- Harmony training: 60%\n- Final optimization: 200 calls\n- Population maintenance: 4 fallback candidates\n- Termination condition: 100s stagnation or 5 successive failures\n- Warm starts from initial solution\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Verify h[i] \u2208 [0,1] via clipping\n2. Ensure \u222bh dx =1 (within 1e-5)\n3. Compute C\u2085 via sliding window correlation\n4. Return:\n   - Linearly sampled final piecewise representation\n   - Negative log overlap (minimized to > -2.3)\n   - n_points=160 (fixed by initial_candidates)",
